    docs(admission): the gate does not validate an 8 x floor ceiling (CF-169)
    
    docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md:44-46 told operators that an enabled satellite
    "must have an explicit family floor and ceiling (8 x floor)" and that
    "the scheduler/API validates the row before it is written or enabled".
    Neither half is true of the shipped scheduler.
    
    The gate refuses exactly one shape -- the fully unarmed row:
    
      internal/api/lane_onboarding.go:269
        func laneIsUnarmed(adaptive bool, floor, ceiling int) bool {
                return !adaptive && floor <= 0 && ceiling <= 0
        }
      internal/api/lane_onboarding.go:303
        func validateLaneArming(name string, enabled, adaptive bool, floor, ceiling int) error {
                family := laneSatelliteFamily(name)
                if family == "" || !enabled || !laneIsUnarmed(adaptive, floor, ceiling) {
                        return nil
                }
                return unarmedLaneError(name, family)
        }
    
    and the pair is only derived on a disable->enable transition of such a row
    (laneAutoArm, :389-403, via DefaultAdaptiveCooldownCeiling ->
    internal/database/models.go:39,47-52, multiplier 8). A row that already
    carries any non-zero bound is written as given and is never refused for its
    ratio. Measured against the live scheduler DB this tick, 12 of the 17
    code-factory rows with a ceiling carry 16x, 48x, 192x, 224x or 576x and all
    are enabled:
    
      code-factory-docs-qa   floor 21600   ceiling 4838400   224x
      code-factory-dogfood   floor  3600   ceiling 2073600   576x
      code-factory-sync      floor  3600   ceiling  172800    48x
    
    The same section's provenance paragraph said the foreman's "six-hour
    fallback" was live and that no durable pin existed in ~/.hermes/fleet.toml.
    Measured now: cooldown_s=1800, admission_mode=tasks, and the loaded
    fleet.toml carries the code-factory stanza with cooldown_s = 1800. Both
    sentences are corrected, and the live floor/ceiling divergence is left with
    CF-134, which owns the cadence values, rather than restated as a gate.
    
    Six claims in the same neighbourhood were re-run and are TRUE (layer-scope
    fixture rows, --layers rejection arms, node-create --staged-files set, the
    family pin table against satelliteFamilyPins, bunker-server-install.sh having
    no --help branch, and the selftest gate). They are recorded in the evidence
    file so the next docs tick does not re-derive them.
    
